                <pb facs="0768" n="648" sameAs="frus:frus1905/pg648"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Clipping from the Daily Record, City of Mexico,
                        November 29, 1905.</hi></head>
                <p>All warlike Yaquis are shot wherever captured, but those who are merely
                    sympathizers are being deported, family by family, as fast as the troops can
                    round them up. In this way it is hoped that the warriors may be starved into
                    submission. Some of these families go to Yucatan to work on plantations; others
                    go to Sinaloa, where the Redo estate is colonizing to <pb facs="0769" n="649"
                        xml:id="pg649"/> work them on their sugar plantation on the Redo peninsula,
                    while others are being used in convict gangs in public improvements for
                    Sonora.</p>
                <p>The government is disposed to act fairly by those who will become peaceable
                    colonists and is giving them farming implements, animals, and poultry in
                    Sinaloa. Those who will not be colonized are sent to Yucatan, where the rigors
                    of the climate frequently end their lives.</p>
            </div>
